  "scope": "Only move PRF word next-value generation out of the monolithic next_state block into independent fixed-row always_comb blocks. No added register or clock boundary; same WB ports and prf_d issue bypass.",
  "preserved_semantics": [
    "Every nonzero row defaults to its previous prf_q word.",
    "Apply WB lanes in the original increasing order, under wb_valid && ROB.writes_rd && fault==0 && destination==row. The last matching lane retains priority.",
    "Row0 is always zero; its old end-of-next_state override moves into the fixed row0 block.",
    "ready_d/free_d updates, rename/commit ordering, and prf_d bypass reads remain in the original next_state locations."
  ],
  "dependency_review": [
    "wb_valid and wb_result are produced by writeback_arbitration from candidate and execute_error.",
    "candidate/execute_error are produced from registered EX/ROB/LQ state, registered MDU responses and cache responses. They do not depend on ready_d/free_d/prf_d or newly selected ex_d.",
    "Recovery eligibility uses registered EX/ROB/LQ state. MDU request selection uses registered ex_q.",
    "The new direction is completed-WB -> fixed-row prf_d -> next_state issue operands -> registered ex_q next clock. It does not feed the current-cycle completed-WB producers.",
    "Strict default Verilator lint and the assertion-enabled simulator build completed without UNOPTFLAT, latch or multiple-driver warnings. This is dependency review plus lint/regression evidence, not a full sequential equivalence proof."
  ],
